"Internal Error ()\nciteturn183view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2010-title2/html/USCODE-2010-title2-chap26-sec1603.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - TCPL AIF 2009 _v6_.doc (https://www.tcenergy.com/siteassets/pdfs/investors/shareholders/historical-information/tcpl-regulatory-filings/2009/tcpl-2009-annual-information-form.pdf)\nciteturn183view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 87; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn182view0\",\"pattern\":\"2003\"}); Total lines: 4648\nL136@P3: incorporated pursuant to the provisions of the Canada Business Corporations Act on February 25, 2003. The\nL137@P3: arrangement was approved by TCPL common shareholders on April 25, 2003 and following court approval, Articles of\nL138@P3: Arrangement were filed making the arrangement effective May 15, 2003. The common shareholders of TCPL\nL139@P3: exchanged each of their TCPL common shares for one common share of TransCanada. The debt securities and preferred\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - TCPL AIF 2009 _v6_.doc (https://www.tcenergy.com/siteassets/pdfs/investors/shareholders/historical-information/tcpl-regulatory-filings/2009/tcpl-2009-annual-information-form.pdf)\nciteturn183view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 87; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn182view0\",\"pattern\":\"all of the issued\"}); Total lines: 4648\nL191@P3: NOVA Gas\nL192@P3: Transmission Ltd.\nL193@P3: Alberta\nL194@P3: 701671 Alberta Ltd.\nL195@P3: Alberta\nL196@P3: TransCanada Hungary\nL197@P3: Liquidity Management LLC\nL198@P3: Hungary\nL199@P3: Global Infrastructure\nL200@P3: Holdings Ltd.\nL201@P3: Bermuda\nL202@P3: TransCanada Energy Ltd.\nL203@P3: Canada\nL204@P3: TransCanada Energy\nL205@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL206@P3: Canada\nL207@P3: TransCanada American\nL208@P3: Investments Ltd.\nL209@P3-4: DelawareTRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 2\nL210@P4: The above diagram does not include all of the subsidiaries of TCPL. The assets and revenues of excluded subsidiaries in the\nL863@P17: of 270 MW, subject to the necessary approvals. Cartier Wind is 62 per cent owned by TCPL. All of the power\nL864@P17: produced by Cartier Wind Energy Project is sold to Hydro-Québec Distribution under a 20-year power purchase\nL865@P17: agreement. In fourth quarter 2009, the proposed 150 MW Les Méchins wind farm project was cancelled due to\nL866@P17: unavailability of cost-effective wind turbines and difficulty reaching acceptable agreements with private landowners.\nL867@P17: This decision has no impact on the other Cartier Wind Energy projects.\nL868@P17: • A 683 MW natural gas-fired power plant near the town of Halton Hills, Ontario is under construction and is expected\nL869@P17: to be placed in service in the third quarter of 2010. All of the power produced by the facility is contracted to be sold\nL870@P17: to the Ontario Power Authority under a 20-year clean energy supply contract.\nL871@P17: • The Coolidge generating station is a simple-cycle, natural gas-fired peaking power generation station under\nL876@P17: Agricultural Improvement and Power District under a 20-year power purchase contract.\nL877@P17: • The 132 MW Kibby wind power project is under construction and is planned to include 44 turbines located in Kibby\nL878@P17: and Skinner townships in Maine. Construction began in July 2008 and commissioning of the first phase occurred in\nL879@P17: October 2009 with half the turbines operational and a generating capacity of 66 MW, and the second phase which\nL880@P17: consists of the remaining 22 turbines is expected to go into service in 2010 with a generating capacity of 66 MW.\nL881@P17: Further information about TCPL's energy holdings and significant developments and opportunities relating to energy can be found\nL882@P17: in the MD&A under the headings \"Energy\", \"Energy – Highlights\", \"Energy –Financial Analysis\", and \"Energy – Opportunities\nL883@P17-18: and Developments\". TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 16\nL884@P18: GENERAL\nL885@P18: Employees\nL1031@P21: All of TCPL’s common shares are held by TransCanada and as a result, any dividends declared by TCPL on its common shares\nL1032@P21: are paid to TransCanada. TCPL’s Board has not adopted a formal dividend policy. The Board reviews the financial performance\nL1033@P21: of TCPL quarterly and makes a determination of the appropriate level of dividends to be declared in the following quarter.\nL1034@P21: Provisions of various trust indentures and credit arrangements to which TCPL is a party, restrict TCPL’s ability to declare and pay\nL1050@P21: outstanding at Year End, and an unlimited number of first preferred shares and second preferred shares, issuable in series. There\nL1051@P21: were 4,000,000 Series U and 4,000,000 Series Y first preferred shares issued and outstanding at Year End. The following is a\nL1052@P21: description of the material characteristics of each of these classes of shares.\nL1053@P21: Common Shares\nL1054@P21: As the holder of all of TCPL’s common shares, TransCanada holds all the voting rights in those common shares.\nL1055@P21: First Preferred Shares, Series U\nL1056@P21: Subject to certain limitations, the Board may, from time to time, issue first preferred shares in one or more series and determine for\nL1057@P21: any such series, its designation, number of shares and respective rights, privileges, restrictions and conditions. The first preferred\nL1058@P21: shares as a class, have, among others, provisions to the following effect.\nL1064@P21: TCPL is entitled to purchase for cancellation, some or all of the first preferred shares, Series U outstanding at the lowest price\nL1065@P21: which such shares are obtainable, in the opinion of the Board, but not exceeding $50.00 per share plus costs of purchase.\nL1066@P21: Furthermore, TCPL may redeem, on or after October 15, 2013, some or all of the first preferred shares, Series U upon payment for\nL1067@P21-22: each share at $50.00 per share. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED 20\nL1068@P22: Except as provided by the Canada Business Corporations Act or as referred to below, the holders of the first preferred shares will\nL1069@P22: not have any voting rights nor will they be entitled to receive notice of or to attend shareholders’ meetings unless and until TCPL\nL1070@P22: fails to pay, in the aggregate, six quarterly dividends on the first preferred shares, Series U.\nL1071@P22: The provisions attaching to the first preferred shares as a class may be modified, amended or varied only with the approval of the\nL1387@P26: Resources Committee can be found on TransCanada's website under the Corporate Governance - Board Committees page located\nL1388@P26: at www.transcanada.com. Information about the audit committee can be found in this AIF under the heading \"Audit Committee\".\nL1389@P26: Further information about the Board committees and corporate governance can also be found on TransCanada’s website.\nL1390@P26: Officers\nL1391@P26: All of the executive officers and corporate officers of TCPL reside in Calgary, Alberta, Canada. Current positions and offices held\nL1655@P31: 5,537 DSUs and 2,214 DSUs, respectively, which were granted quarterly, in arrears, based on the closing price of the common shares of TransCanada at\nL1656@P31: the end of each quarter in 2009 of $29.83, $31.32, $33.37 and $36.19, respectively.\nL1657@P31: Directors are entitled to direct all or a portion of their cash retainers, meeting fees and travel fees to be paid in DSUs. In 2009, Mr.\nL1658@P31: Benson, Mr. Burney, Dr. Draper and Mr. Hawkins directed all of their retainers, meeting fees and travel fees to be paid in DSUs.\nL1947@P37: \"Other Public Board Committee Memberships\". TCPL believes that due to the specialized nature of the industry, it is important\nL1948@P37-38: for its Board to be composed of qualified and knowledgeable directors. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED B-2\nL1949@P38: In 2009, independent directors of the Board met separately before and after every regularly scheduled and special meeting. There\nL1950@P38: were eight regularly scheduled meetings and one special meeting during 2009. In addition, all of the directors are available to\nL2084@P40: The annual assessment and individual director's terms of reference are then used in the evaluation of the contribution of individual\nL2085@P40: directors.\nL2086@P40: Formal interviews with each member of TCPL's executive leadership team are carried out annually by the Chair. The Chair of the\nL2087@P40: Governance Committee also interviews each director annually on his or her assessment of the Chair's performance. Each of these\nL2088@P40: assessments are reported annually to the full Board. The Governance Committee monitors and discusses external assessments of\nL2089@P40-41: Board governance and regularly monitors the literature on evolving best practice in corporate governance. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED B-5\nL2090@P41: Financial Literacy of Directors\nL2091@P41: The Board has determined that all of the members of its Audit Committee are financially literate. An individual is financially\nL2092@P41: literate if he or she has the ability to read and understand a set of financial statements that present a breadth and level of\nL2093@P41: complexity of accounting issues that are generally comparable to the breadth and complexity of the issues that can reasonably be\nL2094@P41: expected to be raised by TCPL's financial statements.\nL2095@P41: Majority Voting for Directors\nL2096@P41: TransCanada has adopted a policy whereby, at any meeting where the number of nominees for election is the same as the number\nL2097@P41: of director positions on the Board, if proxy votes withheld for the election of any particular director are greater than 5% of the\nL2098@P41: votes cast by proxy, a ballot pertaining to the election of each of the directors will be held at that meeting. A director is required to\nL2099@P41: tender his resignation if the director receives more votes \"withheld\" than \"for\" that director's election when such ballot is held. In\nL2105@P42: CHARTER OF THE AUDIT COMMITTEE\nL2106@P42: 1. Purpose\nL2107@P42: The Audit Committee shall assist the Board of Directors (the “Board”) in overseeing and monitoring, among other\nL2108@P42: things, the:\nL2109@P42: • Company’s financial accounting and reporting process;\nL2110@P42: • integrity of the financial statements\nL2111@P42: • Company’s internal control over financial reporting;\nL2112@P42: • external financial audit process;\nL2113@P42: • compliance by the Company with legal and regulatory requirements; and\nL2114@P42: • independence and performance of the Company’s internal and external auditors.\nL2115@P42: To fulfill its purpose, the Audit Committee has been delegated certain authorities by the Board of Directors that it may\nL2116@P42: exercise on behalf of the Board.\nL2117@P42: 2. Roles and Responsibilities\nL2118@P42: I. Appointment of the Company’s External Auditors\nL2119@P42: Subject to confirmation by the external auditors of their compliance with Canadian and U.S. regulatory registration\nL2120@P42: requirements, the Audit Committee shall recommend to the Board the appointment of the external auditors, such\nL2121@P42: appointment to be confirmed by the Company’s shareholders at each annual meeting. The Audit Committee shall also\nL2122@P42: recommend to the Board the compensation to be paid to the external auditors for audit services and shall pre-approve the\nL2123@P42: retention of the external auditors for any permitted non-audit service and the fees for such service. The Audit Committee\nL2124@P42: shall also be directly responsible for the oversight of the work of the external auditor (including resolution of\nL2125@P42: disagreements between management and the external auditor regarding financial reporting) for the purpose of preparing\nL2126@P42: or issuing an audit report or related work. The external auditor shall report directly to the Audit Committee.\nL2127@P42: The Audit Committee shall also receive periodic reports from the external auditors regarding the auditors’ independence,\nL2184@P43: (e) ensure the internal auditor has access to the Chair of the Audit Committee and of the Board and to the Chief\nL2185@P43: Executive Officer and meet separately with the internal auditor to review with him any problems or difficulties he\nL2186@P43: may have encountered and specifically:\nL2187@P43: (i) any difficulties which were encountered in the course of the audit work, including restrictions on the scope of\nL2188@P43: activities or access to required information, and any disagreements with management;\nL2189@P43: (ii) any changes required in the planned scope of the internal audit; and\nL2190@P43: (iii) the internal audit department responsibilities, budget and staffing;\nL2191@P43-44: and to report to the Board on such meetings; TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED C-3\nL2192@P44: V. Insight in Respect of the External Auditors\nL2193@P44: (a) review the annual post audit or management letter from the external auditors and management’s response and follow\nL2194@P44: up in respect of any identified weakness, inquire regularly of management and the external auditors of any\nL2195@P44: significant issues between them and how they have been resolved, and intervene in the resolution if required;\nL2196@P44: (b) review the quarterly unaudited financial statements with the external auditors and receive and review the review\nL2197@P44: engagement reports of external auditors on unaudited financial statements of the Company;\nL2198@P44: (c) receive and review annually the external auditors’ formal written statement of independence delineating all\nL2199@P44: relationships between itself and the Company;\nL2200@P44: (d) meet separately with the external auditors to review with them any problems or difficulties the external auditors may\nL2201@P44: have encountered and specifically:\nL2202@P44: (i) any difficulties which were encountered in the course of the audit work, including any restrictions on the scope\nL2203@P44: of activities or access to required information, and any disagreements with management; and\nL2204@P44: (ii) any changes required in the planned scope of the audit;\nL2205@P44: and to report to the Board on such meetings;\nL2206@P44: (e) review with the external auditors the adequacy and appropriateness of the accounting policies used in preparation of\nL2207@P44: the financial statements;\nL2208@P44: (f) meet with the external auditors prior to the audit to review the planning and staffing of the audit;\nL2209@P44: (g) receive and review annually the external auditors' written report on their own internal quality control procedures; any\nL2210@P44: material issues raised by the most recent internal quality control review, or peer review, of the external auditors, or\nL2211@P44: by any inquiry or investigation by governmental or professional authorities, within the preceding five years, and any\nL2212@P44: steps taken to deal with such issues;\nL2213@P44: (h) review and evaluate the external auditors, including the lead partner of the external auditor team;\nL2214@P44: (i) ensure the rotation of the lead (or coordinating) audit partner having primary responsibility for the audit and the audit\nL2215@P44: partner responsible for reviewing the audit as required by law, but at least every five years;\nL2216@P44: VI. Oversight in Respect of Audit and Non Audit Services\nL2217@P44: (a) pre-approve all audit services (which may entail providing comfort letters in connection with securities\nL2218@P44: underwritings) and all permitted non audit services, other than non audit services where:\nL2219@P44: (i) the aggregate amount of all such non audit services provided to the Company constitutes not more than 5% of\nL2220@P44: the total fees paid by the Company and its subsidiaries to the external auditor during the fiscal year in which the\nL2221@P44: non audit services are provided;\nL2222@P44: (ii) such services were not recognized by the Company at the time of the engagement to be non audit services; and\nL2223@P44: (iii) such services are promptly brought to the attention of the Audit Committee and approved prior to the\nL2224@P44: completion of the audit by the Audit Committee or by one or more members of the Audit Committee to whom\nL2225@P44: authority to grant such approvals has been delegated by the Audit Committee;\nL2226@P44: (b) approval by the Audit Committee of a non audit service to be performed by the external auditor shall be disclosed as\nL2227@P44: required under securities laws and regulations;\nL2228@P44: (c) the Audit Committee may delegate to one or more designated members of the Audit Committee the authority to\nL2229@P44: grant pre-approvals required by this subsection. The decisions of any member to whom authority is delegated to\nL2230@P44: pre-approve an activity shall be presented to the Audit Committee at its first scheduled meeting following such pre\u0002approval;\nL2231@P44: (d) if the Audit Committee approves an audit service within the scope of the engagement of the external auditor, such\nL2232@P44: audit service shall be deemed to have been pre-approved for purposes of this subsection;\nL2233@P44: VII. Oversight in Respect of Certain Policies\nL2234@P44: (a) review and recommend to the Board for approval the implementation and amendments to policies and program\nL2235@P44: initiatives deemed advisable by management or the Audit Committee with respect to the Company’s codes of\nL2236@P44-45: business ethics and Risk Management and Financial Reporting policies; TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED C-4\nL2237@P45: (b) obtain reports from management, the Company’s senior internal auditing executive and the external auditors and\nL2238@P45: report to the Board on the status and adequacy of the Company’s efforts to ensure its businesses are conducted and\nL2243@P45: of accounting, internal controls and auditing matters are in place, and receive reports on such matters as necessary;\nL2244@P45: (d) annually review and assess the adequacy of the Company’s public disclosure policy;\nL2245@P45: (e) review and approve the Company’s hiring policies for partners, employees and former partners and employees of the\nL2246@P45: present and former external auditors (recognizing the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 does not permit the CEO,\nL2247@P45: controller, CFO or chief accounting officer to have participated in the Company’s audit as an employee of the\nL2248@P45: external auditors’ during the preceding one-year period) and monitor the Company’s adherence to the policy;\nL2249@P45: VIII. Oversight in Respect of Financial Aspects of the Company’s Pension Plans, specifically:\nL2250@P45: (a) provide advice to the Human Resources Committee on any proposed changes in the Company’s pension plans in\nL2257@P45: (e) approve the appointment or termination of auditors and investment managers;\nL2258@P45: IX. Oversight in Respect of Internal Administration\nL2259@P45: (a) review annually the reports of the Company’s representatives on certain audit committees of subsidiaries and\nL2260@P45: affiliates of the Company and any significant issues and auditor recommendations concerning such subsidiaries and\nL2261@P45: affiliates;\nL2262@P45: (b) review the succession plans in respect of the Chief Financial Officer, the Vice President, Risk Management and the\nL2263@P45: Director, Internal Audit;\nL2264@P45: (c) review and approve the policy and guidelines for the Company’s hiring of partners, employees and former partners\nL2265@P45: and employees of the external auditors who were engaged on the Company’s account;\nL2266@P45: X. Oversight Function\nL2267@P45: While the Audit Committee has the responsibilities and powers set forth in this Charter, it is not the duty of the Audit\nL2268@P45: Committee to plan or conduct audits or to determine that the Company’s financial statements and disclosures are\nL2269@P45: complete and accurate or are in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles and applicable rules and\nL2270@P45: regulations. These are the responsibilities of management and the external auditors. The Audit Committee, its Chair and\nL2271@P45: any of its members who have accounting or related financial management experience or expertise, are members of the\nL2272@P45: Board, appointed to the Audit Committee to provide broad oversight of the financial disclosure, financial risk and control\nL2273@P45: related activities of the Company, and are specifically not accountable nor responsible for the day to day operation of\nL2274@P45: such activities. Although designation of a member or members as an “audit committee financial expert” is based on that\nL2275@P45: individual’s education and experience, which that individual will bring to bear in carrying out his or her duties on the\nL2276@P45: Audit Committee, designation as an “audit committee financial expert” does not impose on such person any duties,\nL2277@P45: obligations or liability that are greater than the duties, obligations and liability imposed on such person as a member of\nL2278@P45: the Audit Committee and Board in the absence of such designation. Rather, the role of any audit committee financial\nL2279@P45: expert, like the role of all Audit Committee members, is to oversee the process and not to certify or guarantee the internal\nL2280@P45: or external audit of the Company’s financial information or public disclosure.\nL2281@P45: 3. Composition of Audit Committee\nL2282@P45: The Audit Committee shall consist of three or more Directors, a majority of whom are resident Canadians (as defined in\nL2283@P45: the Canada Business Corporations Act), and all of whom are unrelated and/or independent for the purposes of applicable\nL2284@P45: Canadian and United States securities law and applicable rules of any stock exchange on which the Company's shares are\nL2285@P45: listed. Each member of the Audit Committee shall be financially literate and at least one member shall have accounting\nL2291@P46: The members of the Audit Committee shall be appointed by the Board from time to time, on the recommendation of the\nL2292@P46: Governance Committee and shall hold office until the next annual meeting of shareholders or until their successors are\nL2293@P46: earlier appointed or until they cease to be Directors of the Company.\nL2294@P46: 5. Vacancies\nL2295@P46: Where a vacancy occurs at any time in the membership of the Audit Committee, it may be filled by the Board on the\nL2296@P46: recommendation of the Governance Committee.\nL2297@P46: 6. Audit Committee Chair\nL2298@P46: The Board shall appoint a Chair of the Audit Committee who shall:\nL2299@P46: (a) review and approve the agenda for each meeting of the Audit Committee and as appropriate, consult with members\nL2300@P46: of management;\nL2301@P46: (b) preside over meetings of the Audit Committee;\nL2302@P46: (c) make suggestions and provide feedback from the Audit Committee to management regarding information that is or\nL2303@P46: should be provided to the Audit Committee;\nL2304@P46: (d) report to the Board on the activities of the Audit Committee relative to its recommendations, resolutions, actions and\nL2305@P46: concerns; and\nL2306@P46: (e) meet as necessary with the internal and external auditors.\nL2307@P46: 7. Absence of Audit Committee Chair\nL2308@P46: If the Chair of the Audit Committee is not present at any meeting of the Audit Committee, one of the other members of\nL2309@P46: the Audit Committee present at the meeting shall be chosen by the Audit Committee to preside at the meeting.\nL2310@P46: 8. Secretary of Audit Committee\nL2311@P46: The Corporate Secretary shall act as Secretary to the Audit Committee.\nL2312@P46: 9. Meetings\nL2313@P46: The Chair, or any two members of the Audit Committee, or the internal auditor, or the external auditors, may call a\nL2314@P46: meeting of the Audit Committee. The Audit Committee shall meet at least quarterly. The Audit Committee shall meet\nL2315@P46: periodically with management, the internal auditors and the external auditors in separate executive sessions.\nL2316@P46: 10. Quorum\nL2317@P46: A majority of the members of the Audit Committee, present in person or by telephone or other telecommunication device\nL2318@P46: that permit all persons participating in the meeting to speak to each other, shall constitute a quorum.\nL2319@P46: 11. Notice of Meetings\nL2320@P46: Notice of the time and place of every meeting shall be given in writing or facsimile communication to each member of\nL2321@P46: the Audit Committee at least 24 hours prior to the time fixed for such meeting; provided, however, that a member may in\nL2322@P46: any manner waive a notice of a meeting. Attendance of a member at a meeting is a waiver of notice of the meeting,\nL2323@P46: except where a member attends a meeting for the express purpose of objecting to the transaction of any business on the\nL2324@P46: grounds that the meeting is not lawfully called.\nL2325@P46: 12. Attendance of Company Officers and Employees at Meeting\nL2326@P46: At the invitation of the Chair of the Audit Committee, one or more officers or employees of the Company may attend any\nL2327@P46: meeting of the Audit Committee.\nL2328@P46: 13. Procedure, Records and Reporting\nL2329@P46: The Audit Committee shall fix its own procedure at meetings, keep records of its proceedings and report to the Board\nL2330@P46-47: when the Audit Committee may deem appropriate but not later than the next meeting of the Board. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED C-6\nL2331@P47: 14. Review of Charter and Evaluation of Audit Committee\nL2332@P47: The Audit Committee shall review its Charter annually or otherwise, as it deems appropriate, and if necessary propose\nL2468@P51: A member is independent if the member has no direct or indirect relationship which could, in the view of the Board,\nL2469@P51: be reasonably expected to interfere with the exercise of a member’s independent judgment.\nL2470@P51: C. Directors who are not members of management will meet on a regular basis to discuss matters of interest\nL2471@P51: independent of any influence from management.\nL2472@P51: D. Certain of the responsibilities of the Board referred to herein may be delegated to committees of the Board. The\nL2478@P51: Section IV. Subject to these legal obligations and to the Articles and By-laws of the Company, the Board\nL2479@P51: retains the responsibility for managing its own affairs, including:\nL2480@P51: i) planning its composition and size;\nL2481@P51: ii) selecting its Chair;\nL2482@P51: iii) nominating candidates for election to the Board;\nL2483@P51: iv) determining independence of Board members;\nL2484@P51: v) approving committees of the Board and membership of directors thereon;\nL2485@P51: vi) determining director compensation; and\nL2605@P55: 5. a detailed look at the decisions the Human Resources Committee of the Board of Directors (the \"HR Committee\") made\nL2606@P55: with respect to the compensation of the Executive Officers in light of the Company’s performance in 2009.\nL2607@P55: INTRODUCTION\nL2608@P55: The executive compensation program for TransCanada is managed by the Board of Directors with guidance from the HR\nL2609@P55: Committee. The objective of the executive compensation program is to provide compensation that is competitive, fair, and\nL2610@P55: supportive of the Company’s business plans, delivered in such a manner as to be consistent with the best interests of all\nL2611@P55: shareholders. The nature of TransCanada’s business impacts the way in which performance is assessed. This performance\nL2612@P55: assessment, in turn, directly impacts how compensation is delivered over time.\nL2613@P55: TransCanada’s businesses are capital intensive, many are subject to regulated returns and growth is typically driven by projects\nL2617@P55: has been particularly evident during the past few years and will continue to be applicable in the future with the prospects of new\nL2618@P55: large capital projects being considered. The Company is also mindful of the importance of dividends to shareholders and the need\nL2619@P55: for a balance between current returns, a conservative capital structure and long term growth.\nL2620@P55: The Board recognizes that compensation programs that primarily reward delivery of short term returns could be detrimental to\nL2621@P55: investment in a stream of projects and actions that could promote longer term growth in the value of the Company and growing\nL2622@P55: returns to investors. However, in the other extreme, excessive focus on longer term projects could decrease the Company’s ability\nL2623@P55: to generate current earnings, pay dividends, and maintain access to the capital markets. The Board has carefully considered a\nL2624@P55: balanced approach to these issues in the design of the executive compensation program and the impact of compensation systems\nL2625@P55: on business risk.\nL2626@P55: The Board establishes meaningful performance objectives for management for both the short and long term compensation plans.\nL2627@P55: In establishing these objectives, the Board understands that important elements of executive performance cannot be measured\nL2628@P55: entirely through financial measures. For example, the management of projects under development or under construction is critical\nL2629@P55: to the value of the Company, and the assessment of performance in that regard can be subjective rather than based on numerical\nL2630@P55: measurements. Another important element of performance is how well the management team meets the Company’s objectives\nL2631@P55: with respect to health, safety and the environment. The Board has a rigorous process of both setting these objectives as well as\nL2632@P55: assessing the performance of all executives in consideration of subjective and objective measurement. The final determination of\nL2633@P55: performance is made based on a combination of specific financial measures and the assessment of the other elements of\nL2634@P55: management’s responsibility.\nL2635@P55: Although constantly seeking improvements to the design and administration of TransCanada’s executive compensation program,\nL2636@P55: the HR Committee and the Board are confident that the current systems and practices are in the best interest of the shareholders\nL2637@P55: and have operated as they were designed. Shareholders should expect the Board to use the Company’s compensation resources\nL2638@P55: wisely to build long term value creation. TransCanada’s Board believes that the Company’s compensation philosophy and\nL2639@P55-56: executive compensation program are consistent with this expectation. TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED F-2\nL2640@P56: SUMMARY FOR 2009\nL2641@P56: In evaluating 2009 overall corporate performance, the HR Committee and the Board considered a number of qualitative and\nL2642@P56: quantitative factors including financial and share performance, the quality of earnings, execution of on-going projects and\nL2643@P56: transactions, safety, operational performance and progress on key growth initiatives. For 2009, the HR Committee and the Board\nL2644@P56: were of the opinion that the Company performed well and met or exceeded expectations in most areas. This was demonstrated by:\nL2645@P56: • Cash provided by operations grew 20 per cent to $3.4 billion in 2009 compared to $2.8 billion in 2008;\nL2646@P56: • Acquired ConocoPhillips’ remaining interest in Keystone, increasing TransCanada’s ownership to 100 per cent;\nL2647@P56: • Completed construction of the first phase of Keystone to Wood River and Patoka, Illinois;\nL2648@P56: • Entered into an arrangement with ExxonMobil Corporation for the joint development of the Alaska pipeline and in\nL2654@P56: For 2009, the HR Committee and the Board concluded that overall, the Company met its performance objectives but also\nL2655@P56: recognized below-expectation performance of total shareholder return. This assessment did not trigger any specific awards for the\nL2656@P56: Executive Officers but served to provide general context for the review by the HR Committee and the Board of the Executive\nL2657@P56: Officers’ individual performance. They also recognized that uncertainties in the global economy and volatility in the world capital\nL4193@P80: Under the terms of the Stock Option Plan, the maximum number of common shares reserved for issuance as stock options to any\nL4194@P80: one participant in any fiscal year cannot exceed 20% of the total number of options granted in that fiscal year. Additionally, the\nL4195@P80: number of common shares that may be reserved for issuance to insiders, or issued to insiders within any one year period, under all\nL4196@P80: of TransCanada's security based compensation arrangements cannot exceed 10% of TransCanada's issued and outstanding\nL4299@P82: on compensation above approximately $156,000 per annum.\nL4300@P82: Supplemental Pension Plan\nL4301@P82: All of the Pension Plan Employees, including the Executive Officers, who have Pensionable Earnings over the Income Tax Act\nL4302@P82: (Canada) ceiling of approximately $156,000 per year, participate in the Company's non-contributory defined benefit supplemental\nL4303@P82: pension plan. Approximately 500 employees currently participate in the supplemental pension plan.\n"
